This document is the narrative companion to the machine-readable changelog. Where the changelog lists what changed, this document records why those changes happened and what alternatives were considered and rejected. Both documents are authoritative, and the narrative version exists for readers who need context rather than line items.

Version one point zero shipped on an otherwise ordinary afternoon in spring. The release arrived eleven sessions after the first commit and was tagged with three days of calendar time behind it. The unusual compression of calendar time was a consequence of three factors working together. The first factor was a commitment to writing every design decision down as it was made, which meant almost no time was spent revisiting settled ground. The second factor was a deliberate choice to scope each session tightly rather than ambitiously. The third and most important factor was an unusual kind of pair programming in which the human author worked alongside an agent whose strengths complemented the author's weaknesses. The first release contained a working parser, a language server, a type checker, an intermediate representation, an optimization pipeline, a satisfiability-backed refinement checker, five output formats, and a reproducible release pipeline. It did not contain everything the original design sketch called for, but every feature it did contain was tested, documented, and frozen under a stability commitment.

Day two was the most productive of the three. In the morning the type checker was completed, including a bidirectional inference engine with row polymorphism. By early afternoon the intermediate representation was lowering cleanly from the typed abstract syntax tree. By evening the satisfiability infrastructure was wired in, though the actual solver binaries were not yet installed. The key insight of day two was that the validation backlog would grow faster than the implementation backlog if validation was deferred. A half-day detour on day three to install both solvers and validate the satisfiability pipeline end-to-end was the only thing that made the subsequent optimization work trustworthy.

One bug stands out from the release cycle. The audit chain signature code had a subtle flaw in how it extracted string fields from JSON. Backslashes in file paths were not being unescaped before hashing, which meant the chain verification would fail for any path containing a Windows-style separator. The bug would have shipped silently if it had not been caught during the codegen work in the final session. Every claim of reproducibility the release made depended on that chain verifying cleanly. This is the kind of bug that argues for end-to-end validation at every milestone. A test that exercises the chain with realistic path data would have caught it earlier, and a test was added after the fact.

Eleven sessions in three days is not a normal cadence. It is not sustainable indefinitely. It was possible this time because the scope was bounded, the author was focused, and the collaboration was unusually smooth. Future work on this repository will not attempt to sustain that pace, and readers should not infer from this release that the author is a superhuman programmer. A single productive collaboration does not predict the next one.

The twelfth session closed several research threads. The thirteenth is expected to focus on polish work and on expanding the evaluation corpus. The fourteenth and beyond will branch depending on how the release is received and on whether the sibling project begins consuming the artifacts shipped here.

If you are reading this document, the release has reached someone who cares enough to read the narrative changelog. Thank you for that, because the project exists because small acts of care compound.
